<h2>The dispute: a 1952 agreement, a 1954 letter, a 1960 sale deed</h2>
<p>The property was a plot of roughly 4,132 square yards in Friends Colony, New Delhi. The plaintiffs claimed through Sohan Singh, who held an <strong>agreement to sell dated 14 January 1952</strong> from the colonising society. Clause 7 of that agreement required construction on the plot within three years. By a <strong>letter dated 4 October 1954</strong>, Sohan Singh himself asked the society to transfer the plot to Gurbax Singh, whom he described as his nephew. The society executed a <strong>registered sale deed dated 3 December 1960</strong> in favour of Gurbax Singh. The plaintiffs sued for possession only in <strong>1975</strong>, before the Additional District Judge, Tis Hazari.</p>
<p>The Trial Court dismissed the suit. The Delhi High Court affirmed in RFA No. 377/2010 by judgment dated 2 February 2012. The Supreme Court has now affirmed both, dismissing the civil appeal that had remained pending since 2012.</p>

<h2>What the Supreme Court held</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Limitation</h4><p>The suit was held "hopelessly barred by limitation" under Articles 54, 59 and 64 of the Limitation Act, 1963, measured against the 1960 registered sale deed and the long-known 1954 letter.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Possession simpliciter</h4><p>Where a serious dispute casts a cloud over the plaintiff's title, a suit for possession alone is not maintainable; a declaration — cancellation of the sale deed or specific performance — was essential and was never sought.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Agreement to sell</h4><p>An agreement to sell "does not confer any right title or ownership" absent a registered sale deed; property worth more than Rs. 100 passes only by registered conveyance under Section 54 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Estoppel and acquiescence</h4><p>The 1954 letter, asking the society to transfer the plot to the defendant, estopped the plaintiffs under Section 115 of the Evidence Act, 1872 from setting up a contrary case; the predecessor had unequivocally acquiesced.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The maintainability point: when possession needs a declaration</h2>
<p>The core procedural teaching lies in paragraphs 48 to 53 of the judgment. A plaintiff in settled possession-based litigation has, broadly, three templates: a suit based on <em>prior possession</em> against a trespasser; a suit for possession based on <em>title</em>; and a suit for <em>declaration of title with consequential possession</em>. The choice is not free. Where the defendant holds a registered instrument in his favour — here, the 1960 sale deed — the plaintiff's title is not merely denied but clouded by a competing document of title. In that situation the plaintiff must directly attack the instrument or establish his own title by declaration. A bare prayer for possession invites dismissal, because the court cannot deliver possession to a party whose ownership remains judicially unresolved against a registered deed.</p>
<p>The practical drafting lesson for plaint-writers is to audit the defendant's likely documentary position <em>before</em> framing the reliefs. If a registered deed, mutation backed by a deed, or a decree stands in the defendant's way, the plaint must seek declaration or cancellation, pay court fee accordingly, and reckon with the shorter three-year limitation that such reliefs attract under Articles 54, 58 and 59 — rather than hoping the twelve-year window of a possessory claim will carry the case.</p>

<h2>Agreement to sell: a contract, not a conveyance</h2>
<p>The Court reiterated that an agreement to sell creates only a personal obligation; ownership passes by registered sale deed alone. The holder of an agreement has remedies — specific performance within limitation, and in defined circumstances the statutory shield of part performance — but not ownership. On <strong>Section 53A</strong> of the Transfer of Property Act, the Court found the plaintiffs had not satisfied the ingredients, including possession referable to the contract and continuous readiness and willingness; paragraphs 64 to 67 record the finding that "the Plaintiffs have not made a case of part-performance". Clause 7 of the 1952 agreement — the obligation to construct within three years — had itself been abandoned, a point the Court tied to its finding of acquiescence.</p>

<h2>Estoppel by one's own letter</h2>
<p>Few features of the case were as decisive as the 4 October 1954 letter. A party's own prior written statement, acted upon by others, became the hinge: under Section 115 of the Evidence Act, 1872, the plaintiffs could not approbate the letter's request that the society transfer the plot to the defendant and simultaneously reprobate the sale deed that implemented it. The fraud plea fared no better; the Court noted that "no cogent evidence was brought" to establish fraud in the 1960 deed.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>For litigants in Delhi's property courts, Rano Singh is a reminder that stale paper rights rarely survive three filters applied together: limitation computed from the adverse registered document, the maintainability requirement of a declaratory relief where title is clouded, and the estoppel generated by the claimant's own historical correspondence. Each filter is routinely tested at the framing-of-issues stage and in first appeals before the Delhi High Court.</p>
</div>
